gm-method (a wide taskkill filter used to tear down ONE temporary throwaway process can kill an unrelated live user session): `taskkill //F //FI "PID gt 0"` (or any filter matching "any process with a PID", which is every process) is not a scoped kill -- it terminated a project's supervisor process and, as a side effect via process-tree/port ownership, an entirely separate user-started dev server on a different port. When tearing down a temporary diagnostic process (e.g. a throwaway A/B-comparison server on a scratch port), find and kill it by its OWN specific PID (from `netstat -ano | grep :<port>` or the PID the background-launch command reported), never by a broad filter expression -- broad filters silently take out sibling/unrelated processes with no warning. If a broad kill turns out to have hit something unintended, the recovery is immediate and honest: check what's down (`netstat`, health-check the expected port), restart the affected process if it's safe to do so (a stateless dev server), and tell the user directly rather than silently patching over it -- especially if AskUserQuestion goes unanswered, restoring a broken state to working is the safe default action, not leaving it down.